# ======================================================================================
# 050 — ORFMOS HOME DESKTOP v0.2.93
# Main Home desktop + nested Applications, Developer Tools, Files, Settings, and Game Lab / Library desktops.
# R70 preserves R69 VM-state polling decoupling and adds a self-sufficient Drive byte-provider fallback.
# Drive target ingestion no longer depends exclusively on the shared _drive_read_bytes global; Home resolves the active BIOS Drive service directly when that helper is absent.
# VM state remains low-level and menu-neutral: ADB transport + guest boot identity + sys.boot_completed. PackageManager is probed only by capabilities that require it.
# R73 introduces the ORFMOS MPS INSTALL transaction boundary: LOCATE -> READ -> VALIDATE -> WRITE. APK bytes are staged into the guest before PackageManager is invoked, separating ADB byte exchange from package realization.
# R74 adds a resident-runtime probe bridge: UI buttons invoke allow-listed probe IDs inside the same Colab runtime that owns ORFVM, ADB, package state, and staged artifacts.
# R75 expands that bridge with bounded package-provider path diagnostics: cmd package list/path and pm path are observed separately, with invocation cause/identity carried in the VM contract trace.
# R76 adds resident guest-command channels. Existing Android commands are carried through long-lived ADB shells, with bounded stimulus probes used only when a command stalls; state-changing input is never blindly replayed.
# R77 starts successor-frame observation immediately after input dispatch so visual reaction, command acknowledgement, and stimulus evidence overlap instead of serializing their latency.
# R78 adds read-only SystemUI/framework responsiveness tomography so InputDispatcher, SurfaceFlinger, ActivityManager, WindowManager, PackageManager, and com.android.systemui are classified independently of VM liveness.
# R79 treats the Android VM as a compatibility execution surface and compresses machine-observation handling into one canonical OBSERVATION control; copy/export serialize the underlying data model rather than rendered DOM.
# R79 also keeps OBSERVING_REACTION presentation transactions owned by one observer, preserves the last good frame through slow guest reactions, and converts soft refresh timeout into a recoverable waiting state.
# R80 makes the VM presentation navigation bar ORFMOS-owned: Back returns to Developer Tools, Home returns to ORFMOS Home, and Recents opens an ORFMOS recent-tools/applications surface.
# R80 adds a client-continuity recent-access registry with scrollable cards and a small provider-neutral registration API so future ORFMOS tools/apps can participate without VM-specific code.
# R80 also claims the visible guest status strip as ORFMOS shell chrome: Settings -> ORFMOS Settings, Wi-Fi -> Network diagnostics, signal -> Peer Network, and battery -> System / Client Hardware Profile.
# R81 adds contextual shell-return ownership: Recents and status-strip surfaces opened from the Android VM return to that VM surface instead of a hard-coded parent.
# R82 redraws Android SystemUI ANR presentation as an ORFMOS-owned compatibility prompt. A bounded read-only SystemUI guard observes the guest provider while the ORFMOS shell remains interactive.
# R83 promoted the Special Access reference APK into an ORFMOS-native intent surface; R84 corrects that presentation boundary by making VM Module Permissions the canonical Settings surface and retaining Android only as a compatibility/reference provider.
# R84 adds per-module ORFMOS policy state for presentation/runtime settings and compatibility permissions, provider-scoped authority semantics, compact-landscape Settings behavior, and legacy Special Access routing into Settings -> VM Module Permissions.
# R85 consolidates the proven XML/AXML -> OXML -> executable-manifest -> ACF/ORFVM -> MPS VALIDATE chain. AndroidManifest.xml source encoding is normalized before package semantics enter Installer state; PackageManager remains WRITE realization only.
# R85.1 repairs MPS guest-stage byte/hash validation with direct argv guest commands. The observed 97-byte mismatch was bare-wc line-count parsing, not APK corruption.
# R85.2 decouples resident Home generation from selector generation during presentation refresh. A verified resident generation newer than the selector is served without silent downgrade; device presentation preferences remain independently rehydrated.
# R86 promotes the Colab resident-runner workflow into Developer Tools -> Preflight Validator: device-local click/file-picker ingress on mobile, click or drag/drop on desktop, immutable byte/SHA staging, explicit resident Python execution, APK handoff through the existing Installer prepare contract, and registered non-WRITE validation probes.
# R86.1 repairs Preflight interaction inheritance at the Home-layer boundary: the entire active Preflight surface explicitly accepts pointer/touch input, all controls remain interactive, scroll panels use touch pan-y behavior, and device ingress uses a transparent native file-input overlay for reliable mobile tap while retaining desktop drag/drop.
# R86.2 adds a backwards-compatible COPY OUTPUT bridge for the Preflight Validator. The latest observation/result is serialized as formatted JSON and copied via navigator.clipboard when available, with a legacy hidden-textarea/document.execCommand('copy') fallback for older Android/WebView environments.
# R86.3 captures resident execution stdout/stderr and verification-report globals into the Preflight execution envelope. COPY OUTPUT therefore carries the verifier's actual detailed result rather than only the outer execution-complete wrapper, while preserving the existing resident-global execution model and console visibility.
# R87 promotes Developer Tools -> Module Creator into a notebook-style native surface. Module identity, symbolic path declarations, ordered path-load setup, and module source are edited as cells. VALIDATE is non-WRITE; BUILD MODULE emits immutable Python + notebook/spec artifacts with readback SHA provenance and never executes or publishes the result automatically.
# R88 wires Developer Tools -> Manifest Creator directly to immutable Module Creator output. Built modules are discovered by artifact SHA/spec provenance; the selected module may be handed directly from Module Creator. Manifest validation/build is deterministic and non-executing, producing a stable ORFMOS module manifest with module/spec identity, declared path/load semantics, capabilities, and provenance.
# R89 implements Developer Tools -> Runtime Recovery as the immutable Home version selector. Durable selector snapshots are immutable version records; SET ACTIVE validates the selected record, referenced BIOS manifest, and Home 050 bytes/SHA, snapshots current selector authority, mutates only the known mutable selector file, and verifies Drive readback. Resident development generations are visible but never selector-eligible without a durable manifest record.
# R90 promotes Developer Tools -> Device Compatibility into the deployable system-probe catalog. Probe artifacts are immutable embedded payloads with exact byte/SHA identity, exposed through read-only download routes. The initial OPTIPLEX_LINUX_READINESS family provides Windows PowerShell and Linux/live-USB realizations to collect browser-oriented hardware facts without installing or changing software.
# R90.1 consolidates Device Compatibility into SYSTEM PROBES / ANDROID · ORF CONSOLE / RESULTS tabs. Android tomography is represented by one immutable ORF Console probe-suite package rather than duplicating its internal probes in the outer catalog. The ORF Console APK is pinned by Drive ID, bytes and SHA256; download performs live Drive readback and exact integrity verification before returning the package.
# R90.2 makes Home link generation self-hosted. /orfmos/home/current serves the current resident Home assets directly from the resident FastAPI runtime, /orfmos/home/current-link generates a same-origin externally usable link, and the Home LINK control copies that URL. Manifest refresh now treats browser presentation state independently from server presentation state: a new/stale client is always given current verified CSS/JS even when the resident server already knows that generation is current.
# R90.3 promotes the existing Developer Tools -> ChatGPT tile into the ORFMOS/ChatGPT integration landing surface. The surface observes the independently versioned resident MCP Runtime Gateway, shows bounded connector/tool/Home identity, copies the current Home link, and reveals the full MCP capability URL only after explicit REVEAL_MCP_ENDPOINT authorization. Normal state remains masked and no connector execution/write authority is added.
# R90.4 integrates the temporary HTTPS GET compatibility bridge into the ChatGPT landing. Home refresh remains presentation-only; the landing now observes bridge residency explicitly and can reveal/copy the bridge discovery URL only under explicit REVEAL_TEMP_WEB_BRIDGE authorization.
# R90.25 makes UI Lab target selectors visibly styled on mobile, adds a dedicated full-screen drag editor, and commits validated surface+viewport layouts into /content/current as disposable current projections.
# R90.26 turns that editor into user-authorized session presentation: explicit portrait/landscape/desktop template spaces, client-profile selection, render-target selection, individually movable shell/app controls, and an always-visible session commit dock.
# R90.28 derives the editor interaction horizon from the live client visual viewport, auto-selects portrait/landscape/desktop from client class + orientation, replaces fragile native editor selects with touch-owned pickers, exposes the actual render-target selector inside the editor, and adds a redundant top CLOSE control.
# R90.29 removes the modal editor state: UI Lab is the editor. Its interaction-horizon bar owns TARGET UI, TEMPLATE, and EDITOR SETTINGS dropdowns; Close controls are retired, UI EDITOR is itself a selectable target, and COMMIT CURRENT installs the verified projection into the live session projection layer so the next/active render of that surface uses the committed geometry.
# R90.31 restores visual continuity in the inline editor footer and adds REVERT DEFAULT beside COMMIT CURRENT. Revert clears the user-session projection for the selected target/template/client, verifies /content/current removal when resident, restores canonical template geometry, and removes live projection overrides without touching durable Home authority.
# R90.32 preserves Home icon visual identity during committed layout projection: Home CARD nodes are positional anchors only, so live session apply moves each native Home button without stretching its intrinsic dimensions into editor-card outlines.
# R90.33 adds viewport maximize/restore ownership to security/settings panels. The control uses the live visualViewport while maximized and restores the panel to its current responsive presentation without mutating session layout or durable authority.
# R90.34 corrects that ownership target: viewport maximize/restore belongs to the UI editor stage, not Security/Permissions. Maximized UI Lab occupies the live visualViewport and elevates the Home layer + editor stage to the foremost ORFMOS presentation layer; RESTORE returns to the current responsive inline editor without mutating layout authority.
# R90.35 finishes the current UI-editor core cleanup: maximized editing owns the true top-left dynamic viewport while the unobtrusive ORFMOS/version watermark may remain above it, and Home preview cards reuse the native Home icon presentation instead of generic editor card boxes.
# R90.12 closes the resident-only durability gap: runnable Preflight Python is SHA-addressed into immutable Drive storage before execution, required runtime-service/connector artifacts are exact-readback gated before development selector mutation, and recovered durable services are verified-all-before-execute then hydrated into the shared Home FastAPI runtime. Android ZIP ingress advertises common provider MIME aliases while backend package validation remains authority.
# Android PackageManager remains a realization provider, not ORFMOS package-state authority; provider stalls never demote an otherwise live VM.
# Installer readiness waits while VM liveness remains positive instead of failing on a fixed retry budget; transient service-pipe failures remain recoverable.
# Variable input/output methods remain device-manifest-driven and provider-neutral; Android ADB is only the current binding.
# BIOS CONTINUE resolves this module lazily and replaces the BIOS browser surface.
